---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
const SITE_URL = 'https://atelier.pieper.io';

const MCP_CONFIG = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "storybook-react": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "${SITE_URL}/storybook-react/mcp"
    },
    "storybook-angular": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "${SITE_URL}/storybook-angular/mcp"
    },
    "storybook-vue": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "${SITE_URL}/storybook-vue/mcp"
    }
  }
}`;

const STORYBOOK_LINKS = [
  // border = brand hue (decorative); text color comes from a per-framework
  // CSS class that picks an AA-safe shade per theme.
  { name: 'React',   pkg: '@atelier-ui/react',   href: `${SITE_URL}/storybook-react/`,   border: '#61dafb', textClass: 'storybook-card--react' },
  { name: 'Angular', pkg: '@atelier-ui/angular', href: `${SITE_URL}/storybook-angular/`, border: '#e23237', textClass: 'storybook-card--angular' },
  { name: 'Vue',     pkg: '@atelier-ui/vue',     href: `${SITE_URL}/storybook-vue/`,     border: '#42b883', textClass: 'storybook-card--vue' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Storybook"
  description="How Atelier UI uses Storybook as a live component explorer — stories per framework, MCP-backed docs, and visual regression tests."
  currentPath="/storybook"
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">
    <PageHero
      kind="reference"
      title="Storybook"
      lede="Interactive component explorers for Angular, React, and Vue. Browse stories, inspect props, and test accessibility in isolation."
      ledeMax="520px"
    />

    <!-- Framework cards -->
    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 3rem">
      {STORYBOOK_LINKS.map(({ name, pkg, href, border, textClass }) => (
        <a
          href={href}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          style={`display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem; padding: 1.1rem 1.1rem 0.9rem; background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised); border-radius: var(--ui-radius-md); text-decoration: none; border-left: 3px solid ${border}; transition: background var(--ui-transition-fast)`}
          class={`storybook-card ${textClass}`}
        >
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem">
            <span class="storybook-card__name">{name}</span>
          </div>
          <span style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted)">{pkg}</span>
          <span class="storybook-card__cta">Open</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    <!-- MCP section -->
    <div>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title">MCP server</h2>
      <p style="margin: 0 0 1.75rem; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65">
        All three Storybook instances expose a hosted MCP endpoint so AI coding assistants can read component documentation directly — no local setup required.
      </p>

      <div class="docs-step-list">
        <!-- Step 01 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">01</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">Add the MCP server to your project</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              In your AI assistant's MCP configuration (e.g. <code>.mcp.json</code> at the project root for Claude Code) add one or more servers:
            </p>
            <Code code={MCP_CONFIG} lang="json" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 02 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">02</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">Use it in your prompts</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              Once connected, your AI assistant can look up component props, variants, and usage examples directly from Storybook:
            </p>
            <Code code={`Build a settings page using @atelier-ui/<your framework> components.\nUse the storybook-<your framework> MCP to check available props.`} lang="text" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 03 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">03</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">Two MCP surfaces, three toolsets</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              Storybook ships MCP in two layers — added in 10.4, with the tool surface renamed and the manifests made framework-native at 10.6.0, the version this repo pins. The <strong>hosted</strong> endpoint serves docs from a static manifest — works for any framework, no install. The <strong>local dev</strong> surface adds dev &amp; test tools while a local Storybook is running; under 10.6 it is no longer React-only in practice — a locally-run Angular or Vue Storybook answers the same eight-tool surface React does. Tools are grouped into three <strong>toolsets</strong> (<code>dev</code>, <code>docs</code>, <code>test</code>), each toggleable in the addon options.
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Toolset</th><th>Tool</th><th>Hosted (atelier.pieper.io)</th><th>Local dev (<code>nx storybook &lt;fw&gt;</code>)</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td rowspan="3"><code>docs</code></td><td><code>docs-list</code></td><td>All three: components + docs</td><td>All frameworks</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>docs-show</code></td><td>All three: full, each framework-native</td><td>All frameworks</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>docs-show-story</code></td><td>All three: full, each framework-native</td><td>All frameworks</td></tr>
                <tr><td rowspan="5"><code>dev</code></td><td><code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code></td><td>—</td><td>All three (10.6)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>stories-preview</code></td><td>—</td><td>All three (10.6)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>stories-changed</code> <em>(10.4)</em></td><td>—</td><td>Requires <code>features.changeDetection</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>stories-find-by-component</code></td><td>—</td><td>Requires a builder that exposes the module-graph service</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>review-create</code></td><td>—</td><td>Requires <code>features.changeDetection</code> (and <code>experimentalReview</code> not disabled)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>test</code></td><td><code>test-run</code></td><td>—</td><td>Requires <code>@storybook/addon-vitest</code>; a11y via <code>@storybook/addon-a11y</code></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.75rem">
              The hosted endpoint reads <code>manifests/components.json</code>, which the Storybook build writes once <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> is installed — the addon switches the <code>componentsManifest</code> feature on through its own preset. Since Storybook 10.6, all three framework packages fill that file for real: React via <code>react-docgen</code> (its default all along), Angular via <code>@storybook/angular-vite</code>'s <code>angular-component-meta</code>, and Vue via <code>@storybook/vue3-vite</code>'s <code>vue-component-meta</code> — both behind <code>experimentalDocgenServer</code>, which this repo sets explicitly (ADR-0097, superseding the React-manifest fallback ADR-0083 describes). An Angular or Vue lookup now returns real variants, defaults and state props already shaped for that framework: two-way <code>[(checked)]</code> bindings and split Inputs/Outputs for Angular, <code>v-model</code>/<code>update:*</code> events and typed slots for Vue — no more <code>children</code> or <code>onCheckedChange</code> outside the React endpoint. <code>check:storybook-manifests</code> gates all three builds so a regression back to a decoy or empty manifest fails offline before it reaches the hosted surface.
            </p>
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">
              The dev / test toolsets require a running Node.js Storybook dev server, and under 10.6 that surface answers on all three frameworks — not the React-only preview scope of earlier versions. Their per-tool gates are capability checks rather than framework checks — <code>stories-find-by-component</code> wants a builder that exposes the module graph, <code>stories-changed</code> and <code>review-create</code> want <code>features.changeDetection</code>, <code>test-run</code> wants <code>@storybook/addon-vitest</code>. <code>docs</code> tools support a <code>storybookId</code> parameter to scope a request to one source in multi-source (composition) setups; <code>docs-list</code> accepts <code>withStoryIds: true</code> when downstream tools need story IDs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 04: 10.4 highlights -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">04</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">What Storybook 10.4 added to MCP</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              The 10.4 release — together with the <code>@storybook/mcp</code> versions that shipped alongside it — tightens the agentic loop on six fronts, all still in place at the pinned 10.6.0:
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it does</th><th>How to enable</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>experimentalReactComponentMeta</code> (RCM)</td>
                  <td>Volar + TypeScript Language Server docgen replaces <code>react-docgen</code> / <code>react-docgen-typescript</code> — faster, better detection, faster dev-mode HMR for prop edits. When enabled it is also the engine behind React&apos;s <code>components.json</code>; Atelier leaves it off, so that manifest is built by <code>react-docgen</code> — the file&apos;s own <code>meta.docgen</code> field names whichever engine ran.</td>
                  <td><code>experimentalReactComponentMeta: true</code> in <code>.storybook/main.ts</code> (React). <code>@storybook/mcp@0.6.2+</code> reads the new <code>reactComponentMeta</code> payload automatically.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Change Review sidebar &amp; <code>stories-changed</code></td>
                  <td>Storybook tracks <em>new</em>, <em>modified</em>, and <em>affected</em> stories. The MCP tool returns just that subset so agents can scope review to recent edits.</td>
                  <td>Set <code>features.changeDetection: true</code> in <code>.storybook/main.ts</code>. Tool registers automatically.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Multi-source composition + OAuth</td>
                  <td>Compose several Storybooks behind one MCP endpoint via Storybook refs. The addon serves <code>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource</code> and accepts bearer tokens so agents can reach private composed Storybooks.</td>
                  <td>Add <code>refs</code> to <code>.storybook/main.ts</code>; pass <code>storybookId</code> to docs tools to disambiguate.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>MCP Apps preview resource</td>
                  <td><code>stories-preview</code> exposes a <code>ui://stories-preview/preview.html</code> resource so MCP-Apps-capable hosts render the story preview inline in chat (Cursor, Claude Desktop with MCP Apps). Non-capable hosts get the <code>previewUrl</code> per story instead.</td>
                  <td>Automatic when the host advertises MCP Apps support.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Subcomponent docs</td>
                  <td><code>docs-show</code> surfaces subcomponent props for compound components.</td>
                  <td><code>@storybook/mcp@0.7.0+</code> &amp; <code>@storybook/addon-mcp@0.6.0+</code>. No config.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Self-host API (<code>createStorybookMcpHandler</code>)</td>
                  <td>Reusable docs handler for serving manifests from any runtime (Cloudflare Workers, Netlify Functions, Node). Hooks: <code>manifestProvider</code>, <code>onListAllDocumentation</code>, <code>onGetDocumentation</code>, <code>onSessionInitialize</code>. Tool-registration exports (<code>addListAllDocumentationTool</code>, etc.) for custom <code>tmcp</code> servers.</td>
                  <td><code>import &#123; createStorybookMcpHandler &#125; from '@storybook/mcp'</code>. See <code>worker/mcp.ts</code> in this repo.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 05: Toolset config -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">05</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3" id="toolset-configuration">Toolset configuration</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              All three toolsets default to <code>true</code>. Disable a toolset when an agent should not see those tools — e.g. read-only agents:
            </p>
            <Code code={`// .storybook/main.ts
import type { StorybookConfig } from '@storybook/react-vite';

const config: StorybookConfig = {
  addons: [
    {
      name: '@storybook/addon-mcp',
      options: {
        toolsets: {
          dev: true,    // stories-preview, get-storybook-story-instructions, stories-changed
          docs: true,   // docs-list, docs-show, docs-show-story
          test: false,  // disable test-run for read-only agents
        },
      },
    },
  ],
  features: {
    componentsManifest: true,             // gates the manifest write + docs toolset
    changeDetection: true,                // gates stories-changed
  },
};

export default config;`} lang="ts" />
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">
              Telemetry is on by default — disable with Storybook&apos;s <code>core.disableTelemetry</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 06 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">06</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">Endpoints</h3>
            <table class="docs-props-table" style="margin-top: 0.25rem">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Library</th><th>Hosted URL</th><th>Local dev URL</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>@atelier-ui/react</code></td><td><code>{SITE_URL}/storybook-react/mcp</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:4401/mcp</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>@atelier-ui/angular</code></td><td><code>{SITE_URL}/storybook-angular/mcp</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:4400/mcp</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>@atelier-ui/vue</code></td><td><code>{SITE_URL}/storybook-vue/mcp</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:4402/mcp</code></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">
              In a clone of the atelier monorepo, each framework's Storybook binds its own port — angular
              4400, react 4401, vue 4402 — so all three can run at once; read the exact port from the
              terminal that started it. A scaffolded workspace ships only one framework, and its Storybook
              binds the single port <code>6006</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CLI section -->
    <div>
      <h2 id="cli" class="docs-section-title">CLI</h2>
      <p style="margin: 0 0 1.75rem; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65">
        Everything above is MCP. The <code>storybook</code> binary itself is bigger than that, and almost none of the rest is what this repo actually runs — Nx wraps <code>dev</code> and <code>build</code>, and Vitest replaces the test command entirely. What follows was read from the installed <code>storybook@10.6.0</code> package's own dispatcher and command registration (<code>node_modules/storybook/dist/bin/</code>, plus <code>@storybook/cli@10.6.0</code> fetched on demand), not from release notes, and exercised directly against this repo everywhere marked <strong>verified</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="docs-step-list">
        <!-- Step 01 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">01</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">One command, two packages</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              <code>storybook</code>'s own <code>bin/dispatcher.js</code> inspects <code>argv[0]</code> before anything else: <code>dev</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>index</code>, <code>ai</code>, <code>tools</code> and <code>skills</code> import <code>storybook/dist/bin/core.js</code> directly — the exact 10.6.0 already sitting in this repo's <code>node_modules</code>, nothing to fetch. Every other subcommand is handed to a <em>separate</em> package, <code>@storybook/cli</code>, pinned to the identical version but <strong>not a dependency of this repo</strong> — dispatcher.js tries to resolve it, fails, and falls back to running it through the package manager's remote-package path. Verified: <code>npx storybook doctor</code> here fetches <code>@storybook/cli@10.6.0</code> from the registry on first use, the same way <code>npx storybook@10.6.0 doctor</code> would in a project that had never installed Storybook at all. One footgun this split creates: plain <code>storybook --help</code> only ever shows the <code>@storybook/cli</code> command list (<code>init</code>, <code>add</code>, …) — <code>dev</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>index</code>, <code>ai</code>, <code>tools</code> and <code>skills</code> never appear there, because dispatcher.js only checks <code>argv[0]</code> against that fixed list and <code>--help</code> doesn't match it, so it falls through to the other package. Each of the six has its own working <code>--help</code>; the top-level one just doesn't list them.
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Command</th><th>Package</th><th>Needs</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>dev</code></td><td><code>storybook</code> (local)</td><td>nothing beyond <code>node_modules</code></td><td>Dev server. This repo's <code>nx storybook &lt;fw&gt;</code> target.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>build</code></td><td><code>storybook</code> (local)</td><td>nothing beyond <code>node_modules</code></td><td>Static build; with <code>componentsManifest</code> on, also writes <code>manifests/components.json</code>. This repo's <code>nx build-storybook &lt;fw&gt;</code> target.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>index</code></td><td><code>storybook</code> (local)</td><td>nothing beyond <code>node_modules</code></td><td>Writes a lightweight story index (titles, story IDs, tags, import paths) via <code>StoryIndexGenerator</code> — no docgen, no manager, no preview bundle, and no relation to <code>manifests/components.json</code> despite the similar name (Step 06).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ai</code></td><td><code>storybook</code> (local)</td><td>nothing beyond <code>node_modules</code></td><td>Deprecated — its own <code>--help</code> says "AI agent helpers for Storybook (deprecated — see <code>storybook skills</code>)".</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>tools</code></td><td><code>storybook</code> (local)</td><td>nothing beyond <code>node_modules</code>; some tools need a running Storybook</td><td>CLI door into the same toolset <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> serves over HTTP (Step 03).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>skills</code></td><td><code>storybook</code> (local)</td><td>nothing beyond <code>node_modules</code></td><td>Prints agent instructions generated from the target Storybook's own config. Not the tool ADR-0123 uses (Step 04).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>init</code></td><td><code>@storybook/cli</code> (network)</td><td>registry fetch, then a package install</td><td>Scaffold Storybook into a project without it. Long-standing, not used here.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>add &lt;addon&gt;</code> / <code>remove &lt;addon&gt;</code></td><td><code>@storybook/cli</code> (network)</td><td>registry fetch + install</td><td>Install or uninstall one addon and wire <code>main.ts</code>. Long-standing, not used here.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>upgrade</code></td><td><code>@storybook/cli</code> (network)</td><td>registry fetch + install — <strong>even with <code>--dry-run</code></strong> (Step 05)</td><td>Bump every Storybook package to the CLI's own version, then run automigrations and <code>doctor</code>. Long-standing, not used here.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>automigrate [fixId]</code></td><td><code>@storybook/cli</code> (network)</td><td>registry fetch; no install unless a fix actually succeeds</td><td>Detect and optionally apply known upgrade fixups. Long-standing, not used here.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>doctor</code></td><td><code>@storybook/cli</code> (network)</td><td>registry fetch only — read-only otherwise (Step 05)</td><td>Static dependency/config health check. Long-standing, not used here.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>info</code> / <code>migrate</code> / <code>sandbox</code>|<code>repro</code> / <code>link</code></td><td><code>@storybook/cli</code> (network)</td><td>registry fetch; varies</td><td>Environment report; standalone codemods; template sandboxes; contributor repro linking. Long-standing, not used here.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 02 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">02</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">What's new for the CLI at 10.5 → 10.6</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              Three changes, all confirmed against the installed dist rather than the changelog:
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Change</th><th>Evidence</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>storybook ai</code> deprecated in favour of <code>storybook skills</code></td>
                  <td>Verified: the <code>ai</code> command's own <code>--help</code> prints the deprecation notice; <code>ai setup</code>'s says "deprecated: use <code>storybook skills setup</code>".</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>storybook tools</code> made public</td>
                  <td>Storybook's own 10.6.0 release material describes it as newly public (announced, not independently re-verified beyond the command existing and working in this dist). Structurally it is real: <code>bin/dispatcher.js</code> routes <code>tools</code> the same way it routes <code>dev</code>/<code>build</code>, and it answers with a full tool surface (Step 03).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A new <code>automigrate</code> fix, <code>addon-mcp</code>, fires whenever any AI coding agent is detected</td>
                  <td>Verified by reading the fix (<code>src/automigrate/fixes/addon-mcp.ts</code>) and by running it (Step 05): its <code>check()</code> calls <code>detectAgent()</code> and returns non-null — meaning "applicable" — whenever an agent is detected, <em>regardless of whether <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> is already installed at the correct pinned version</em>.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 03 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">03</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3"><code>storybook tools</code> — the CLI door into the MCP toolset</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              <code>storybook tools</code> exposes almost the same functions as <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code>, callable from a plain shell — useful for Codex or the Antigravity CLI, which have no MCP client wired to this repo's Storybooks (<code>.mcp.json</code> only speaks Claude Code's MCP protocol). Verified command and output, run against this repo without a dev server:
            </p>
            <Code code={`$ npx storybook tools --config-dir libs/react/.storybook --json docs list
{ "manifests": { "componentManifest": { "components": {
  "components-inputs-atlbutton": { "id": "…", "name": "AtlButton", "reactDocgen": { … }, … }
  # … one entry per component, real props inline
} } } }`} lang="text" />
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">
              Note the option order: global flags (<code>--config-dir</code>, <code>--json</code>, <code>-o</code>) go <em>before</em> the toolset and tool name — <code>tools --config-dir … docs list</code>, not <code>tools docs list --config-dir …</code> (verified the hard way: the latter silently falls back to <code>./.storybook</code> and fails).
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table" style="margin-top: 0.75rem">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>CLI subcommand</th><th>MCP tool it matches</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>docs list</code> / <code>docs show</code> / <code>docs show-story</code></td><td><code>docs-list</code> / <code>docs-show</code> / <code>docs-show-story</code></td><td>local — config dir only (real docgen for React; not for Angular/Vue without a running server, Step 06)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>stories changed</code> / <code>stories find-by-component</code></td><td><code>stories-changed</code> / <code>stories-find-by-component</code></td><td>local</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>stories preview</code></td><td><code>stories-preview</code></td><td>requires a running Storybook</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>review create</code></td><td><code>review-create</code> (hyphenated MCP id; two words as a CLI subcommand)</td><td>requires a running Storybook</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>test run</code></td><td><code>test-run</code></td><td>local, needs <code>@storybook/addon-vitest</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><em>(none)</em></td><td><code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code></td><td>MCP-only — the CLI's equivalent is <code>storybook skills stories</code>/<code>write-story</code> instead (Step 04), a different mechanism, not a <code>tools</code> subcommand</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 04 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">04</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">Two tools, same word: <code>storybook skills</code> vs. <code>skills add</code></h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              Easy to conflate, verified as genuinely different:
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th></th><th><code>npx storybook skills [id]</code></th><th><code>npx skills add storybookjs/mcp</code></th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Package</td><td><code>storybook</code> itself (10.6.0, already installed)</td><td>a third, unrelated npm package literally named <code>skills</code> (pinned <code>1.5.25</code> per ADR-0123)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>What it does</td><td>Prints markdown instructions — <code>stories</code>, <code>write-story</code>, <code>setup</code> — assembled live from <em>this</em> target Storybook's own config (framework, feature flags)</td><td>Clones the <code>storybookjs/mcp</code> GitHub repo and installs its <code>SKILL.md</code> files into <code>.claude/skills/</code> (or another agent's skill directory)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Writes files?</td><td>No — stdout, or <code>-o &lt;path&gt;</code> if asked</td><td>Yes — that is its entire job</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Used in this repo?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes — <code>libs/create-workspace</code>'s preset shells out to exactly this command post-scaffold (ADR-0123)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 05 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">05</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3">Candidates weighed for this repo</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              <strong><code>doctor</code> — worth adding, cheap.</strong> Verified by running it (<code>npx storybook doctor --config-dir libs/react/.storybook</code>: "Your Storybook project looks good!"). It statically checks five things — missing Storybook dependency, incompatible packages, mismatched Storybook versions, duplicated dependencies, and config-load errors — reading <code>package.json</code>/<code>node_modules</code>/<code>main.ts</code>. No build, no dev server. The only cost is the same one every <code>@storybook/cli</code> command pays here: a registry fetch, since the package isn't vendored (Step 01) — a real cost for an offline gate chain, and the reason it isn't proposed as a <code>check:all</code> step without first vendoring <code>@storybook/cli</code> as a dependency.
            </p>
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.75rem">
              <strong><code>automigrate</code> / <code>upgrade</code> — not for this repo, and riskier than the flags suggest.</strong> Verified by running <code>automigrate --dry-run</code> against this repo (safe: every fix's <code>run()</code> gates its writes on <code>dryRun</code>, confirmed by reading every fix in <code>@storybook/cli</code>'s bundle). Even on an already-current, exact-pinned 10.6.0 setup it found two applicable fixes: <code>addon-mcp</code> — which would re-install <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> to "latest" purely because it detects an AI coding agent running, not because the pin is wrong — and <code>wrap-getAbsolutePath</code>, which would rewrite all three <code>.storybook/main.ts</code> files' addon/framework references into <code>getAbsolutePath(...)</code> call expressions because it detects an Nx monorepo. Neither is something this repo wants applied unattended. <code>upgrade</code> is worse: reading its source (not executed, by design) shows <code>--dry-run</code> only gates the package.json version bump and the automigration writes — <code>packageManager.installDependencies()</code> runs unconditionally in its control flow regardless of <code>--dry-run</code>, so even the "check-only" flag can touch <code>package-lock.json</code> and <code>node_modules</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 06 -->
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">06</span>
          <div class="docs-step-body">
            <h3 class="docs-h3" id="manifest-shortcut">The question that matters most: is there a cheaper path to <code>components.json</code>?</h3>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              <code>check:storybook-manifests</code> runs a full <code>nx build-storybook</code> for all three frameworks before it checks anything, which is why it's one of the slower gates. Four candidate shortcuts, each tried directly against this repo — the answer is <strong>no</strong>, with evidence:
            </p>
            <table class="docs-props-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Candidate</th><th>Result</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>storybook index</code></td><td>Reads its implementation (<code>build-index.ts</code>): calls <code>presets.apply('stories'/'docs'/'features'/'experimental_indexers')</code> and nothing that touches manifests. Produces <code>index.json</code> — a story index, not component docgen. Structurally cannot produce <code>components.json</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>storybook build --preview-only</code> (skips the manager UI shell)</td><td>Produces a <code>manifests/components.json</code> of identical size (255,350 bytes, both runs, same component count) at a measured <strong>~11.8s vs ~11.6s for Angular</strong> and ~6.9s vs ~6.0s for React — noise-level, not a real win, because the manager UI was never the expensive part; the docgen-heavy preview compile still runs in full. (The flag's actual documented purpose is an iframe-only preview for unsupported browsers, not build speed — repurposing it bought nothing here anyway.)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>storybook tools --config-dir … docs list</code> (no build, no server)</td><td>For <strong>React</strong>: real inline <code>reactDocgen</code> props per component, in ~seconds. For <strong>Angular</strong>: every component comes back as <code>id</code>/<code>name</code> only — no docgen, no <code>$ref</code> — the exact "decoy" shape <code>check-manifests.js</code>'s own <code>[DECOY]</code> check exists to catch. <code>experimentalDocgenServer</code> (Angular, Vue) needs the real build or a running dev server; the static CLI path can't produce it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Local dev server (<code>nx storybook &lt;fw&gt;</code>) instead of a build</td><td>Doesn't write a static file either way: <code>registerManifests()</code> explicitly 404s the <code>components</code>/<code>docs</code> routes in dev when docgen-server mode is on (Angular, Vue) — by design, per the installed source. React's dev route would serve it live, but not to a file <code>check-manifests.js</code> could read, and the gate needs all three frameworks.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="docs-step-desc" style="margin-top: 0.75rem">
              Since the gate is uniform across all three frameworks and the two that matter most (Angular, Vue) have no shortcut, the existing mitigation — Nx caching <code>build-storybook</code>'s declared outputs, so a rerun with no source change is fast — stays the right answer. Fragmenting the gate to fast-path React alone would trade a real, if partial, speedup for a gate that no longer checks all three the same way.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

